      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The early 1900s Last Chance Flume diverted Bear River water to irrigate Gem Valley farms, a landmark of Idaho’s rural engineering.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitgraceidaho.com/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A few miles outside Grace, the Niter Ice Cave is a naturally chilled lava tube that showcases the region’s volcanic history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitgraceidaho.com/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Oregon Trail ran just 4 miles from Grace, linking the town to the westward migration of the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace%2C_Idaho",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lake Bonneville once reached its northernmost advance about 4 miles southwest of Grace before spilling into the Great Basin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace%2C_Idaho",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The flat floor of Gem Valley around Grace was carved by volcanic eruptions roughly 30,000 years ago, contrasting the nearby mountains.",
